  "text": "To determine how many values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds, we need to analyze the behavior of the greedy algorithm and compare it to other possible coin combinations.\n\n### Step-by-Step Reasoning:\n\n1. **Greedy Algorithm Behavior**:\n   - The greedy algorithm starts by using the largest coin (25-cent coins) and then moves to the next largest coin (10-cent coins), and finally uses 1-cent coins if necessary.\n   - The algorithm will stop once the total value exceeds \\( N \\).\n\n2. **Checking for Greedy Success**:\n   - We need to check if there is any other combination of coins (using 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins) that sums to \\( N \\) using fewer coins than the greedy algorithm.\n   - If no such combination exists, the greedy algorithm is successful for \\( N \\).\n\n3. **Mathematical Analysis**:\n   - For the greedy algorithm to succeed, the sum of the coins used must be exactly \\( N \\).\n   - If there is any other combination of coins that sums to \\( N \\) using fewer coins, the greedy algorithm fails for \\( N \\).\n\n4. **Special Cases**:\n   - For \\( N = 0 \\), the greedy algorithm fails because there are no coins.\n   - For \\( N = 1 \\), the greedy algorithm fails because the only coin available is 25 cents, which is greater than 1.\n\n5. **General Case**:\n   - We need to check for each \\( N \\) from 1 to 1000 whether the greedy algorithm finds the unique combination of coins that sums to \\( N \\).\n\n### Implementation:\n\nWe can implement a function to check if the greedy algorithm succeeds for each \\( N \\) from 1 to 1000. The function will use a recursive approach to find all possible combinations of coins and compare them to the greedy combination.\n\n```python\ndef find_combinations(n, coins):\n    if n == 0:\n        return [[]]\n    if n < 0 or not coins:\n        return []\n    \n    # Recursive call with 25-cent coin\n    combinations_with_25 = find_combinations(n - 25, coins)\n    \n    # Recursive call with 10-cent coin\n    combinations_with_10 = find_combinations(n - 10, coins)\n    \n    # Recursive call with 1-cent coin\n    combinations_with_1 = find_combinations(n - 1, coins)\n    \n    # Combine all possible combinations\n    return combinations_with_25 + combinations_with_10 + combinations_with_1\n\ndef greedy_algorithm_success(n, coins):\n    greedy_combination = find_combinations(n, coins)\n    if not greedy_combination:\n        return False\n    \n    # Check if the greedy combination is unique\n    unique_combination = find_combinations(n, coins)\n    return len(unique_combination) == 1\n\ndef count_successful_n_values(start, end):\n    coins = [1, 10, 25]\n    count = 0\n    for n in range(start, end + 1):\n        if greedy_algorithm_success(n, coins):\n            count += 1\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of values for which the greedy algorithm succeeds\nsuccessful_n_values = count_successful_n_values(1, 1000)\nprint(successful_n_values)\n```\n\n### Final Answer:\nThe number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
